      "text": "To solve for \\( x + y \\) given the equation \\( x^2 + y^2 = 10x - 6y - 34 \\), we start by rewriting the equation in a more manageable form. First, we rearrange it to group the \\( x \\)-terms and \\( y \\)-terms together:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x + y^2 + 6y = -34\n\\]\n\nNext, we complete the square for both \\( x \\) and \\( y \\). For the \\( x \\)-terms, we take the coefficient of \\( x \\), divide it by 2, and square it:\n\n\\[\n10x \\quad \\text{becomes} \\quad 5^2 = 25\n\\]\n\nThen, we add and subtract 25 inside the equation:\n\n\\[\nx^2 - 10x + 25\n\\]\n\nFor the \\( y \\)-terms, we take the coefficient of \\( y \\), divide it by 2, and square it:\n\n\\[\n6y \\quad \\text{becomes} \\quad 3^2 = 9\n\\]\n\nThen, we add and subtract 9 inside the equation:\n\n\\[\ny^2 + 6y + 9\n\\]\n\nNow the equation looks like this:\n\n\\[\n(x^2 - 10x + 25) + (y^2 + 6y + 9) = -34\n\\]\n\nWe can rewrite each squared term as a perfect square:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = -34 + 25 + 9\n\\]\n\nSimplifying the right-hand side gives:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 + (y + 3)^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nSince both \\((x - 5)^2\\) and \\((y + 3)^2\\) are squares of real numbers, they are both non-negative. The only way their sum can be zero is if each term is individually zero:\n\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad (y + 3)^2 = 0\n\\]\n\nThis implies:\n\n\\[\nx - 5 = 0 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y + 3 = 0\n\\]\n\nSolving these equations gives:\n\n\\[\nx = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad y = -3\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\n\\[\nx + y = 5 + (-3) = 2\n\\]\n\nFinally, we conclude:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{2}\n\\]",
